{
  "example": "In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`. {cite}`requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example is covered by tests, so the judgment is true.\n\n**Scenario.** In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` should give the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`.\n\n**Test references.** Two tests carry `{test}` references to `requirements.references.selector_nested`. Both are in `src/outline/tests.rs`:\n- `rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry` (lines 35–100)\n- `selectors_name_syntactic_ancestry_and_duplicates` (lines 662–706)\n\n**Setup.**\n- The first test uses the exact path `src/lib.rs` and includes the line `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }`, as the example describes.\n- The second test uses `lib.rs`, which still selects the Rust profile by extension.\n\n**Assertions.**\n- Both tests assert that the list of all node selectors contains `\"fn[outer]/fn[inner]\"`, so nesting is checked against the exact expected spelling.\n- The first test also asserts that `outline.resolve(\"fn[outer]/fn[inner]\")` returns a node whose `selector` equals that string, so the selector round-trips.\n- `fn[outer]` is never listed on its own as an expected selector in these tests. The tests still imply it:\n  - `resolve` in `src/outline/mod.rs` walks from the root one segment at a time and matches each child's `segment`. The nested selector therefore cannot resolve unless a child with segment `fn[outer]` exists.\n  - `disambiguate` builds each full selector as `parent.selector + \"/\" + segment`, so the parent's selector must be `fn[outer]`.\n- Other tests confirm the parent selector directly:\n  - `line_queries_find_innermost_and_declared_nodes` in the same file asserts that a function with a nested `inner` is selected as `fn[outer]`.\n  - `search_returns_innermost_nodes_and_comment_owners` in `src/authoring/tools.rs` asserts `fn[outer]/fn[inner]` for a nested function.\n\n**Implementation.** This is context only, since the subject is an example.\n- `KINDS` in `src/outline/rust.rs` maps `function_item` to kind `fn`, named by its `name` field.\n- `Builder::finish` and `disambiguate` in `src/outline/mod.rs` link nodes to their parents and build the `/`-joined selectors.\n- The example cites `requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`, and that requirement's text covers syntactic-ancestry selectors such as `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`. The user documentation in `md/reference.md` lists `fn[outer]/fn[inner]` as an example selector.\n\nNo FIXMEs or acknowledged gaps are involved.",
  "references": "f3c49019e423d681f14511a10dd0ec04ee48b588885830e2e0fe64fed09ff45b",
